[# Section 1 — Hello CX, design philosophy, how to read this guide.
   Authored 2026-05-22.
   Cross-links use [[anchor]] form; the template renderer (Phase 6.12)
   resolves them at emit time. #]

[section id=intro group='Start here' n=1 title='Introduction'

  [child id=hello n=1.1 title='Hello, CX'
    [intro
      """CX is one language for **data** and **code**. The same brackets, the
         same sigils, the same evaluator. You don't pick a templating engine
         for output, a query language for selection, or a config dialect for
         deployment — there is one shape, and you write everything in it."""]

    [example lang=cx
      """[hello greeting='world']"""]

    [body
      """That is a complete CX document. It is also a complete CX program when
         passed to the evaluator — it evaluates to itself. CX is **homoiconic**:
         programs are data; data round-trips through every supported surface
         (JSON, YAML, TOML, XML, Markdown); identity is the SHA-256 of the
         canonical bytes."""]

    [body
      """Here is one screen of CX that exercises every category covered later
         in this guide — data, code, selection, update, and output:"""]

    [example lang=cx
      """[# 1. Data: a small document
         #]
         [users
           [user id=1 email='ada@example.com' active=true]
           [user id=2 email='grace@example.com' active=false]
           [user id=3 email='linus@example.com' active=true]]

         [# 2. Code: select active users with CXPath
         #]
         [?for [in $u //user[= $_@active true]] [yield
           [active-user email=$u@email]]]

         [# 3. Pure-functional update: mark one user verified
         #]
         [?modify $doc //user[= $_@id 1]/@status [set 'verified']]"""]

    [body
      """Three forms, one language. No string concatenation; no AST mismatch
         between selectors and templates; no separate type system for queries.
         The full vocabulary is detailed in [[data]] and [[code]]; this section
         is just the elevator pitch."""]]

  [child id=philosophy n=1.2 title='Design philosophy'
    [intro
      """CX is built on six commitments. Every design choice in the rest of
         this guide — and every choice deliberately deferred — falls out of
         one of them."""]

    [body
      """**1. Data and code share one syntax.** A directive like `[?for]` is
         spelled the same way as a data element like `[user]`. There is no
         "template language" embedded inside a "data language". A program is
         a document; a document is potentially a program. Tooling — parser,
         lexer, AST, tree-sitter grammar, LSP — is shared end-to-end."""]

    [body
      """**2. Projections round-trip.** CX has a canonical surface
         (the bracket form) and projections for JSON, YAML, TOML, XML, and
         Markdown. JSON, YAML, TOML, and XML are lossless in the
         round-trip sense under the `--lossless` flag: CX → JSON → CX
         produces byte-identical canonical bytes. The default JSON
         projection is idiomatic and lossy for mixed content; Markdown
         (and CSV) are lossy by design. The
         normative round-trip rules live in [[surfaces]] and in
         [`spec/03-approved/core/canonical.md`](../../spec/03-approved/core/canonical.md)."""]

    [body
      """**3. Pure-functional updates.** There is no in-place mutation in CX.
         The `[?modify]` directive (see [[modify]])
         takes a document and a CXPath focus, and returns a **new** document.
         Unchanged branches share structure with the original (the standard
         persistent-tree technique). Concurrency is safe by construction —
         no reader ever observes a half-modified tree."""]

    [body
      """**4. Identity via canonical bytes plus SHA-256.** Two CX documents are
         equal iff their canonical bytes are equal, and the document's
         identifier is the SHA-256 of those bytes. This makes caching,
         deduplication, content-addressable storage, and cross-language
         equality checks trivially correct. See [[identity]]."""]

    [body
      """**5. One selector vocabulary everywhere.** CXPath is **the**
         way to point at things — for reading (`select_all`), for iteration
         (`[?for]`), for type-tests in `[?match]`, and for update focus in
         `[?modify]`. It aligns with XPath 3.1 (see the CXPath sections
         of [`spec/03-approved/core/code.md`](../../spec/03-approved/core/code.md)
         and the conformance corpus `conformance/xpath_31_parity.cxd`) so that
         developers coming from the XML ecosystem feel at home, with
         deliberate divergences to keep it safe and CX-shaped."""]

    [# REUSE: the "one rule, no exceptions" framing in commitment 6 below is
       the canonical brand hook — the README hero and docs-site landing source
       from this block rather than restating it in standalone markdown. Keep it
       tight and quotable. The scored comparison and table that follow are
       guide-only; they must not migrate to the brand surface (decision 1c). #]
    [body
      """**6. Orthogonality — one rule, no exceptions.** CX has exactly one
         syntactic form: `[head …]`. Calls, operators, directives, and data
         elements are all that one shape, distinguished only by the head. There
         is no statement/expression divide, no infix, no special slots, and no
         keyword the grammar treats as a privileged exception. Even the
         constructs other languages hard-code — `if`, `lambda`, `quote`,
         `for` — are in CX ordinary forms obeying the same rule. Read it like
         XML, program it like Lisp; CX is more uniform than either, because in
         CX the surface *is* the rule. And the discipline extends from syntax
         to features: every feature must apply uniformly across its natural
         domain or carry a written, justified exception — enforced by the
         UNIFORM review gate in
         [`spec/03-approved/process/spec-authoring-guide.md`](../../spec/03-approved/process/spec-authoring-guide.md)
         §3, not merely aspired to."""]

    [body
      """**Orthogonality in context.** Orthogonality is the *uniform
         application of a small rule set* — features compose without special
         cases. It is not the same as simplicity: a language can be small yet
         riddled with privileged built-ins user code cannot reproduce, or large
         yet principled. The table below ranks CX against the languages CX binds
         to, plus Lisp-1 and Scala, on this one axis. It is illustrative, not a
         leaderboard — and orthogonality is only one axis. Where CX trades it
         away (ecosystem maturity, library breadth) is the honest accounting in
         [[comparison]]."""]

    [table
      [tr [th 'language'] [th 'tier'] [th 'why']]
      [tr [td 'CX'] [td 'Reference'] [td 'One grammar production; every construct is the same [head …] form. No statement/expression split, no infix, no privileged keyword — even special forms obey the one rule.']]
      [tr [td 'Lisp-1 (Scheme)'] [td 'Near-reference'] [td 'Homoiconic S-expressions; single namespace (more uniform than Lisp-2). Loses only because quote, lambda and if are privileged special forms — CX folds even those into the one rule.']]
      [tr [td 'Scala'] [td 'High (uniform model)'] [td 'Everything is an expression; functions are objects; operators are methods; uniform access. Diluted by a large sugary surface — implicits/givens, two dialects.']]
      [tr [td 'Ruby'] [td 'High (uniform model)'] [td 'Smalltalk lineage — everything is an object, everything a message send, operators are methods. Uniform core, non-uniform surface (blocks vs procs vs lambdas, many sugars).']]
      [tr [td 'V'] [td 'Moderate'] [td 'Deliberately minimal, one canonical way to do things. Orthogonality by austerity; still carries privileged built-ins and a partial statement/expression split.']]
      [tr [td 'Kotlin'] [td 'Moderate'] [td 'Expression-oriented (if, when, try are expressions); operators by convention. Primitives-as-objects is a JVM fiction; much surface exists for Java interop.']]
      [tr [td 'Python'] [td 'Moderate'] [td 'Real uniformity via the dunder protocol; but a hard statement/expression wall — def, class and import are statements, lambda is crippled.']]
      [tr [td 'Swift'] [td 'Moderate'] [td 'Genuine protocol-oriented uniformity, but a special-case-dense surface — optionals, guard, defer, property wrappers, result builders.']]
      [tr [td 'Rust'] [td 'Low–moderate'] [td 'Expression-oriented core, but a federation of subsystems with their own rules — ownership, lifetimes, traits plus coherence, a separate macro grammar, async, unsafe. Powerful is not orthogonal.']]
      [tr [td 'C#'] [td 'Low'] [td 'Multi-paradigm, accreted over many versions — LINQ, async, properties, events, pattern matching each a distinct syntax. Statement/expression divide.']]
      [tr [td 'Java'] [td 'Low'] [td 'The classic wart — primitives vs objects — plus special-cased arrays, no operator overloading, erasure generics, statement-heavy ceremony.']]
      [tr [td 'Go'] [td 'Low'] [td 'Simple but not orthogonal — magic built-ins user code cannot replicate (make, append, len, cap, delete), special slice/map/channel semantics, retrofitted generics, error-by-convention.']]
      [tr [td 'TypeScript / JavaScript'] [td 'Lowest'] [td 'Most accreted — this-rebinding, hoisting, prototype vs class, == vs ===; TypeScript layers an entire erased second language over the runtime.']]]

    [note
      """**Two distinctions drive the ranking.** *Simplicity vs
         orthogonality*: Go and V are simple, but Go is not orthogonal — its
         magic built-ins are unreproducible by user code. *Uniform model vs
         uniform surface*: Scala and Ruby have uniform semantic cores but large
         sugary surfaces; CX is the only entry whose surface itself is the
         single rule, because it is homoiconic (see [[homoiconicity]]) — there
         is no gap between model and syntax for uniformity to fall out of."""]]

  [child id=reading n=1.3 title='How to read this guide'
    [intro
      """This guide is the developer-facing reference for CX. Read it
         linearly the first time. After that, sections are independent
         enough to use as topic-specific reference."""]

    [body
      """The flow is data → surfaces → identity → analytics → code →
         bindings → tooling → concepts → migration. Each section calls
         out the normative spec documents — approved specs live under
         `spec/03-approved/` — rather than
         restating them; if you need the formal grammar, error matrix,
         or wire-format details, the guide points you there. Front-matter
         ([[quickstart]]) and back-matter ([[glossary]] / [[faq]] /
         [[comparison]]) flank the numbered sequence."""]

    [list
      [item """[[data]] — the data language: lexical structure, scalars,
              collections, sigils, prose, typed columnar tables, schema
              language, includes. Start here if you've never seen CX."""]
      [item """[[surfaces]] — the canonical CX form and its JSON / YAML /
              TOML / XML / Markdown projections, plus the round-trip
              contract."""]
      [item """[[identity]] — canonical bytes, SHA-256 hashing, the
              ast-bin and data-bin binary wire formats, ID/IDREF
              scoping, capability bits, FFI lifetime."""]
      [item """[[analytics]] — the columnar / streaming binary surface:
              :table data shape, CXCol format, Arrow zero-copy bridge,
              Parquet read/write, ecosystem integrations (DuckDB /
              Polars / pandas / DataFusion / Spark)."""]
      [item """[[code]] — the CX code language: the directive registry, CXPath,
              patterns, bindings, the six core constructs, builtins,
              errors-as-values, services / workers / async, resilience."""]
      [item """[[bindings]] — the C ABI, Layer 1 canonical method set,
              Layer 2 host idiom packs, and per-language quickstarts for
              V, Python, Go, and Rust."""]
      [item """[[tooling]] — LSP, tree-sitter, VS Code, Neovim, Helix,
              `make guide`, the playground."""]
      [item """[[value-model]], [[computation-identity]],
              [[commands-effects]], [[runtime-representation]] — the
              concept arcs: identity, determinism, effects, structural
              sharing, and how values are represented at runtime."""]
      [item """[[migration]] — upgrade guide: renames, retired surfaces,
              the `cx fmt --migrate-predicates` / `--collapse-lets`
              rewriters, the archived binding set."""]]

    [body
      """**Conventions used throughout.** Code blocks tagged `cx` are
         literal CX (data or code; the same shape). Code blocks tagged
         `python`, `go`, `rust`, or `v` are host-language
         binding code. Cross-references to other sections use `[[anchor]]`.
         References to spec documents use ordinary markdown links."""]

    [note
      """The normative grammar lives in
         [`spec/03-approved/formal/grammar.ebnf`](../../spec/03-approved/formal/grammar.ebnf);
         the directive registry is in
         [`spec/03-approved/core/code.md`](../../spec/03-approved/core/code.md);
         the C ABI surface is in
         [`spec/03-approved/core/abi.md`](../../spec/03-approved/core/abi.md).
         This guide is curated learning
         + reference path, not a replacement for those documents."""]]]
